Date: Sat, 22 May 93 09:37:28 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Is Math Random?

Well, the argument seems to be looping, but it's clear that a big problem
is a lack of a good definition for "random".

Chaitin has written, I think, that any incompressible string can be
viewed as random.

Robin points out that anything can be described by an incompressible
string, hence that everything is (in this sense) random.  So it's not
clear what the distinction is between random and non-random things.
Yet we all intuitively recognize such a distinction.

I suppose one way to look at it is whether or not the string is already
compressed.  My message here is not random because it has redundancy.
If perfectly compressed, perhaps it would become random.

So, just because the compressed version of something is random, it may
not follow that the original thing was random.  This would be an escape
from Robin's proof.

In this sense, perhaps it is the incompressible nature of omega that makes
it random.  Pi is compressible, but omega is not.  Therefore, pi is not
random but omega is.

So, this definition would have it that omega is random.  It's not clear
to me that this implies that "math" is random, since I don't know exactly
what "math" includes, or whether the presence of randomness within some
subject area makes the whole subject random.

Hal

