Date: Sat, 22 May 93 15:23:54 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: CHALLENGE: Chaitin and RTFM

First, I'm glad that the response to the first Accountability
Society CHALLENGE was composed with seriousness and effort.
It sets a sterling precedent.  Thanks, Mike!

Now, my response:

One of the major problems with Mike Price's argument is that
he misuses the word _computable_.  This word has a very definite
meaning that every author, computer scientist, and mathematician
I've ever seen uses when writing about the theory of computation.
It's true that no turing machine can output an infinite sequence --
part of the definition of the "computation" performed by a turing
machine requires that the machine halt.  So the universally-used,
standard way of defining a computable number (dating from Turing's
original 1937 paper on the subject) is that there exists a turing
machine that, when given an arbitrarily large input value m, will
output the m'th digit of that number.

Pi is clearly a computable number.  "Computable" and "Pi" are
seemingly meant for each other, as the latter is a wonderfully
instructive illustration of the former.  There are simple
formulae for Pi that makes producing the actual turing machine
that can computes Pi's m'th digit not very difficult.

Mike says that Omega's computability is analogous to Pi's.
This is simply not true.  There is no turing machine that,
given an arbitrary number m, will generate the m'th digit
of Omega's expansion.  This property is exactly what is meant
by an 'uncomputable' number.

Chaitin is NOT saying that Omega is merely difficult to compute.
There is NO procedure for computing it, whereas there IS a
procedure for computing Pi.  If there were such a procedure,
that would be equivalent to solving the halting problem, which
is IN PRINCIPLE (geez, now that McElwain's been booted off the
net, I wonder if there's a law of conservation of capitalized
words...) unsolvable, not just hard.

Note that since the set of computable numbers is clearly
countable and the set of real numbers is not, it's not
too surprising to find an uncomputable one.

I agree with Mike that the Busy Beaver numbers are interesting,
though for the reasons I've just explained, they are not
relevant in this context.  Chaitin himself (gasp, swoon :-)
wrote a brief paper on BB.

In summary, then:  It is incorrect that the computabilities of
Pi and Omega are analogous.  Pi is computable.  Omega is not.
Omega's uncomputability isn't simply a matter of impatience with
slow computers; theorists like Chaitin don't care about such things
and rarely if ever get confused about them (and certainly not
here).  It is impossible to compute.  Any outlook that maintains
that in some odd sense it is possible also must say the same thing
for the solution of the halting problem, which puts it well outside
the bounds of normal use of the vocabulary of theoretical computer
science.

Though Omega cannot be calculated, it can be bound from below, simply
by running lots of turing machines for a very long time and "tallying
up" the ones that halt.

References or further clarification available on request.

