Date: Sat, 22 May 93 15:29:58 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

Perry replies to my entry in the "Extropianly Incorrect Topic of the Week
contest."

>I think the hole in your argument, David, is a confusion of
>engineering with science.
>
>Leonardo Da Vinci first hypothesized that flying was possible in a
>serious way hundreds of years ago, and men unsuccessfully built flying
>machines for centuries before the Wright Brothers did it "right".
>
>Babbage hypothesized that he could build a computer almost a century
>early -- his experiment failed, but eventually we succeeded.
>
>The mere fact that something can be built in theory doesn't mean that
>we yet have complete knowledge needed to perform the activity.

That would be a legitimate reply if I had claimed that the failure of AI to
live up to its promises was a proof of the existence of the soul, but my
claim was only that it was evidence. Surely you would agree that if Babbage
or Leonardo had succeeded, that would be evidence (very strong
evidence--even proof if "succeed" means "we are certain he did it") in
favor of the claim that what they suggested could be done. But the
probability of a proposition being true prior to an experiment with two
possible outcomes must surely be between the probability posterior
conditional on each outcome. So if a successful experiment raises the
probability that the proposition is true (in this case to 1) a failure must
lower it.

Perry goes on to give other reasons for believing that the soul is, in his
elegant term, "an epiphenomenon of a mechanistic system." My point was not
that there was no evidence for that proposition--merely that the
performance of AI so far is evidence against. And his evidence is only
evidence, not proof--most people who believe in the existence of a soul in
any of the usual senses of the term also believe that there is some
connection between the soul and the brain, which is basically what the
experiments he cites demonstrate.

Nick Szabo argues along essentially the same lines, confusing a proof (the
prediction failed, therefore all assumptions must be false) with a
probabilistic argument (the prediction failed, therefore one should lower
the probability one assigns to each assumption).

Dan Davis writes:

>If however, you can explain the failure on particular assumptions which
>you can demonstrate were poor, you eliminate any aspersion on the
>assumption that we can mechanistically produce something with
>the functionality of conscious beings.

If you can show with certainty that an assumption whose truth was necessary
to get a positive result was false, then the failure of the experiment
gives no information about the truth of the other assumptions. I do not
think that is what you are claiming--given that you put it as

>I have my own nominee for the assumption responsible for failure

Robin Hanson, in his reply, states:

>Fifth, the same argument would apply to any other beliefs that this
>community shared, like being more liberal than average, liking Chinese
>food and MIT, etc.

I think he is misunderstanding my argument. He, if I understand him,
interprets me as saying that the history of AI shows that people in the
field are bad at making predictions, one of the predictions they make is
that mind will ultimately be shown to be explainable by mechanism, so their
past failures are evidence that this prediction is wrong. That is a
legitimate argument, but, as he says, "rather weak." It is not the one I
was making.

I was assuming that AI people were (or might be) correctly analysing the
logic of the situation, making a prediction from a set of assumptions--one
of which was that mind is a certain sort of thing. The predictions were
repeatedly falsified. That lowers the probability we should assign to all
of the assumptions (assuming there is none that we now know with certainty
to be false and necessary to the prediction). The assumption that mind is
mechanism was not, so far as I know, a basis for AI people liking Chinese
food.

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


