Date: Sat, 22 May 93 17:38:36 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

This suggestion is exactly the kind of thing that premature claims result in.
When we were discussing the analogy between near-death experiences and neural
network decomposition I pointed out how harmful such premature comparisons
between current simulation results and actual minds can be.  When researchers
describe their work to interested laymen, they often explain things in
qualitative terms that the listener can understand.  In neural network issues
this often means using psychological terminology.  The listener, eager to
understand the concepts, translates this all too literally to mean that the
phenomena are the same.  These assumptions are then extrapolated upon, until
the common opinion is that intelligent machines already exist.  Then, as a
person, or a public, learns more about the issues they become disillusioned
and the pendulum swings the other way.  Cries of "Hoax!" are heard.  People ask
why the home robots are _still_ not available.

Artificial Intelligence is especially susceptible to this kind of hype effect,
partly because it is so exciting, and partly because it is so easy to ornament
even the most mundane results with suggestive language.  Paradigms rise and
fall, new ones growing upon the failures of the old.  But does this show that
the overall endeavor is fundamentally wrong?  If you want to place bets then I
agree that a consistent failure should lower your investments.  But this
attitude would have killed many branches of science that ultimately proved to
be fruitful.  Would one want to lower funding in a branch of mathematics that
didn't yield anything "useful"?

Intelligence is a difficult problem.  It will take years, perhaps centuries, to
understand.  But I think the failures of the last few decades are not so grand
as to show evidence that the very pursuit is unreasonable.  I also guess that
the "failures" you mention are those of computer scientists, who were working
on a much stricter set of assumptions than that of a mechanistic mind.  They
were too quick to assume that since logical statements can be used to represent
interesting concepts then all interesting concepts are inherently implemented
as a logical statement.  Plenty of other fallacies (IMHO) were commited, fueled
by early successes in tasks that were incorrectly deemed to be the hallmark of
intelligence.  But all that aside, computer scientists were not the only ones
studying the mind.  Physiologists and psychologists have for the last century
at least, shown a steady progress in understanding.  Their results have not
been so sexy, and so the layman didn't pay too much attention...

But failure of AI as _evidence_ for a soul?  Oh please.

Paul


