Date: Sat, 22 May 1993 20:57:35 -0500 (EDT)
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: CHALLENGE: Chaitin and RTFM

How to calculate pi completely:

   Compute the infinite sequence 4*arctan(1)

Time to completion: Infinity

How to calculate Omega completely:

   Have an infinite number of turing machines(nanotech) spread across the 
universe in a line. Each turing machine k calculates the kth digit of Omega.
Since they all run in parallel, in infinite time every turing machine
will have halted. You can compute Omega in the same time as Pi.

  Given infinite mass/energy/space/time, Omega is calculable _in principle_.
If you won't grant infinite space/energy to Omega, don't grant infinite time to
calculate Pi -- all irrational numbers are non-computable in reality.

 Now, I know someone will say "But atleast I can calculate n digits of
Pi in a finite time, with Omega, you could get stuck waiting an infinite
amount of time on the first digit!(halting problem)" My response
would be that you can approximate Omega to any degree depending on how long
you are willing to wait.

(aha! A lightbult just went off in my head!)

  By writing this, I now see Derek and Hal's point. There is 
a fundamental difference between Omega and Pi. It's true that it takes
an arbitrarily large time to calculate Pi to n digits (n large). But
with Pi, you have the assurance that the digits leading up to n are
correct. With Omega, you don't have that assurance because every 1-bit in the
binary string is equivalent to a turing machine never halting. 
If the first bit of Omega is a 1, you could get stuck waiting a long time
just to be "sure" that it really was a 1. However, unlike Pi, you could
never really be sure. I can say with 100% certainty that the first
3 digits of Pi are 3.14. 

  For this reason, you can't compute Omega although you can get an
approximation on it.

Any mistakes?

