that the rationals can be put into 1 to 1 correstpondence with the
natural numbers, while the numbers of the continuum cannot.

Turing extended Cantor's diagonalization method to show that some
numbers are uncomputable, that no Universal Turing Machine (he called
it something different, at least early on!) can produce these numbers.

The number of digits is unimportant to the argument. Consider the very
well-behaved rational number "1/3". It's decimal expansion is
0.333333... and, like pi, it cannot be written down on a finite piece
of paper. Likewise, irrational numbers like SQRT(2) = 1.41421356....
have various interesting properties, but are the epitome of
computability (by all definitions of computability used the last 60
years). Further, trancendental numbers (which are also irrational)
like e = 2.71828... are also computable.

So what isn't?

Imagine a sequence of coin tosses or rolls of a die used to construct a number:

"0.835590348892534875357325480....."

This number, unless one is _very lucky_, is not computable, in the
sense of finding a program which will output the nth term. (A smart
aleck program that says "watch the guy tossing the coin and record
what he gets, then output that number" is of course not kosher.)

By "very lucky" I mean that almost all numbers are not related to the
roots of whole numbers, or are the logs of such numbers, or are
"interesting" in a way that the computable numbers tend to be. Almost
any number drawn (or constructed, as with the coin tosses) at random
will have no "structure" and cannot be computed as the output of an
algorithm.

(Gotta be real careful here: given any number or some fixed number of
digits, like the number above without the trailing dots..., an
algorithm may be found, eventually. But it's unlikely. Again, using
the number itself as "data statements" is clearly not kosher.)

(Other noncomputable numbers arise directly out of mathematics, but
the coin-tossing construction makes the points nicely.)

These insights about randomness, and about apparent riddles such as
these:

-All the numbers less than 100 are obviously "interesting." The
numbers below 1000 are, too. But at some point, perhaps at 10,490,331,
the numbers are no longer all interesting. So what's *the smallest
uninteresting number*?

(Hint: the smallest number that is *not* interesting is automatically
supremely interesting---imagine the headline: "341,619 found to be
smallest uninteresting number!")

-What is the smallest number describable in exactly ten words?

(These riddles may not strike a responsive chord in the readers. This
note has to be brief and can't lay the groundwork for why these
versions of the Berry Paradox lead to our modern notions of
completeness, compressibility, randomness, and computability. They are
the recent equivalent of Russell's similar paradoxes (about the
barbers who don't shave themselves, about "this statment is a lie"
and so on.)

Kolmogorov, Chaitin, Levin, and Solomonoff used these and similar
thoughts in the 1960s to develop the information-theoretic approach to
computability. 

A couple of final points:

* Pi is computable. Easily computable. Pi carries almost no
information, in that a very few bits is sufficient to specify pi (via
the many series which yield pi). Another way of putting this is that
pi is very compressible: the sequence "3.14159265358979323846..." is
compressible to "pi." (To split hairs, since I only gave 20 digits of
pi, that sequence would be compressible to "first 20 digits of pi.")

* The Mandelbrot set, often cited as a very complex object (I've heard
the term "infinite complexity" applied to it!), is actually a very
simple object, in terms of algorithmic complexity. A few bits are
sufficient to specify it. However, the generating algorithm when run
for some number of generations produces an object of apparent
complexity, which Charles Bennett calls "logical depth."

(As I posted on last summer, in my second post to this group,
biological objects--such as the estimated "4 gigabytes" in the human
genome--probably have great logical depths....a program (evolution)
run for a very long time. Thus, the "4 gigabytes" should not be taken
as the same as what we store on a hard disk. Not that the bits are
unstorable, mind you, just incredibly hard to "figure out." Hence the
work of Chaitin and others may have profound significance for some
problems of interest to Extropians. In particular, what is the
algorithmic complexity of a self-reproducing machine?)

* Nearly all random strings are uncompressable, something most of us
know intuitively (try running a compression program on noise....).
This may be seen because there "aren't enough bits to go around."
Imagine that we wished to compress a bunch of 32-bit strings into 16
bits, or even 8 bits...there just aren't enough unique addresses!

(Why compression _ever_ works is basic information theory: most
strings we use in our lives are not completely random, that is, they
have structure, redundancy, and perhaps only occupy a fraction of the
entire space of possibilities. For example, if I said I had just
received a message from "mcp," you would likely expand this out to
Michael Clive Price, or Mchl Clv Prc (here I've compressed by deleting
vowells). Some compressions are lossy (e.g., "mcp" can expand back to
several possibilities, such as male chauvinist pig, even in the
context of this list), some are lossless. Most random numbers have no
structure, no redundancy, and so can't be compressed.)

* Why parts of the universe are compressible into laws and relations
is itself a fascinating problem to ponder.

Enough for now.

-Tim


