SCIENTIFIC EVIDENCE OF INDEPENDENCE/CREATIVTY OF CONSCIOUSNESS
-------------------------------------------------------  
      The concept that organizing principles akin to
consciousness are not mere epiphenomena of matter but in fact
"create reality"--that it may well be the very nature and the
purpose of reality--is supported by some suggestive evidence
in the form of findings, hypothesis and theories in physics,
chemistry, biology and psychology.  Below I presse a summary
of such evidence, and quotes from well known scientists who
reflect on the meaning of this evidence.  
  
1.   EVIDENCE OF CONSCIOUSNESS AT SUBATOMIC LEVEL  
(quick over view of subatomic physics)  
  
UNITY OF REALITY  
  
* Relativity Theory and Quantum Theory suggest there is an
intertwined in a "space-time" continuum and that space-time-
matter-energy are not "fundamental entities" but
manifestations of the same "something", something which some
physicists compare to consciousness.  
  
* There is an amazing consistency and unity in nature: all
electrons have the same mass, spin and charge; all atoms of
the same kind -oxygen, hydrogen, etc. - have the same mass or
weight.    
  
* There is ordered complexity throughout reality from the
subatomic level, up through the chemical, the biochemical and
the physiological, suggesting that intelligence and
consciousness are the first things to emerge, not the last, in
its evolution into the human brain.  
  
*  The Aspect Experiment suggests that non-local connections
between atomic particles, even those separated by great
distances exist.  Non-local influences suggest the existence
of non-physical influences of the nature of mind or
consciousness.  
  
*  Time does not seem to be made up of discrete phases - past,
present, future.  Instead, Einstein's insights that time and
space are interdependent, the existence of particles that are
faster than light, and the seeming action of subatomic
particles moving back and forth in time suggest that all time
is happening all at once. This is much like the memory and
imagination of our own consciousness.  It is only our
pragmatic decision to filter out most of time that makes us
believe that there is past, present and future.  
  
* Rupert Sheldrakes morphogenetic fields are also non-local,
cutting across space and linking past and present in time.  At
the subatomic level. (Note: so many Sheldrake quotes, hadn't
gotten around to finding any yet.)  
  
In the 30s physicist Sir James Jeans made a since much quoted
statement.  "Today there is a wide measure of agreement, which
on the physical side of science approaches almost unanimity,
that the stream of knowledge is heading toward a non-
mechanical reality; the universe begins to look more like a
great thought than a great machine.  Mind no longer appears as
an accidental intruder into the realm of matter; we are
beginning to suspect that we ought rather to hail it as the
creator and governor of the realm of matter..." (Sir James
Jeans, "The Mysterious Universe")  
  
Around the same time??  Physicist (biologist?) Arthur
Eddington similarly wrote: "The ultimate stuff of the universe
is mindstuff." (ANYBODY KNOW WHERE AND WHEN HE WROTE IT?)  
  
Noble Prize winning physicist Brian Josephson, wrote about
non-locality: "It raises the possibility that one part of the
universe may have knowledge of another part - some kind of
contact at a distance under certain conditions." (John
Gleidman, "Interview with Brian Josephson," Omni (4, no.10,
July 1982), p.88.)  
  
Physicist John Wheeler writes: "These considerations reveal
that the concepts of spacetime and time itself are not primary
but secondary ideas in the structure of physical theory. 
These concepts are valid in the classical approximation.
However, they have neither meaning nor application under
circumstances when quantum-geometrodynmacal effects become
important.  Then one has to forego that view of nature in
which every event, past, present, or future, occupies its
preordained position in a grand catalog called 'spacetime' 
There is no spacetime, there is no time, there is no before,
there is no after.  The question what happens 'next' is
without meaning.  (John A. Wheeler, "Superspace and the Nature
of Quantum Geometrodynamics," in C. DeWitt and J.A. Wheeler, 
B~ttelle Rencontres, 1967 Lectures in Mathematics and Physics,
W.A. Benjamin: New York, 1968.)  
  
Physicist Jack Sarfatti, who calls the interconnections in the
quantum sea of reality "wormhole" states, "Signals move
through the constantly appearning and disappearing (virtual)
wormhole connections, providing instant communication between
all parts of space.  These signals can be likened to pulses of
nerve cells of a great cosmic brain that permeates all parts
of space.  This is a point of view motivated by Einstein's
general relativity in the form of geometrodynmaics.  A
parallel point of view is given in the quantum theory as
interpreted by Bohm.  In my opinion, this is no accident
because I suspect that general relativity and quantum theory
are simply two complementary aspects of a deeper theory that
will involve a kind of cosmic consciousness as the key
concept."(Jack Sarfatti and Bob Toben, Space-Time and Beyond,
(New York: E.P. Dutton, 1975.)  
  
Nobel winning? Physicist David Bohm was one of the first to
notice the similarities between quantum physics and
consciousness: "We may well now ask whether the close analogy
between quantum processes and our inner experiences and
thought processes is mere coincidence...The remarkable point-
by-point analogy between thought processes and quantum
processes would suggest that a hypothesis relating these two
may well turn out to be fruitful.  If such an hypothesis could
ever be verified, it would explain in a natural way a great
many features of our thinking." (David Bohm, Quantum Theory. 
London: Constable, 1951, p. 169????)  
  
Engineer and philosopher Arthur M. Young described the ongoing
problem in the early 70s: "Cosmology (`the science of the
fundamental causes and processes in things'-Webster) as
interpreted by cosmologists of the present time suffers from
the omission of a term corresponding to consciousness.  To
describe consciousness as an anthropomorphic  attribute alone
is totally inadequate. Consciousness is, rather, an ingredient
which must be inherent in whatever we employ as descriptive of
the universe, be this dimension matter, space or time.  It is
a basic ingredient.  Because we have a knowledge of
consciousness at least as certain as our knowledge of time or
space or mass, it must be given recognition."  (Arthur M.
Young, "Consciousness and Cosmology," Consciousness and
Reality,  Edited by Charles Muses and Arthur M. Young, (New
York: Avon Books, 1972), pg. 151.)  
  
Nobel Prize winning physicist Eugene P. Wigner suggests
science does not yet have the intellectual or experimental
ability to recognize the relation of consciousness to reality.
"Perhaps the relation of consciousness to matter is not too
dissimilar to the relation of light to matter, as it was known
in the last century; matter clearly influenced the motion of
light but no phenomenon such as the Compton effect was known
at that time which would have shown that light can directly
influence the motion of matter.  Nevertheless, the "reality"
of light was never doubted..."  (Eugene P. Wigner, "The Place
