by its bootstraps", is a dynamic web of events that must be
understood as through its own self-consistency, wrote in 1968:
"Carried to its logical extreme, the bootstrap conjecture
implies that the existence of consciousness, along with all
other aspects of nature, is necessary for the self-consistency
of the whole.  (G.F. Chew, " `Bootstrap': A Scientific Idea,"
Science, Vol. 161, (May 23, 1968), pg 763.  (FOR ME: From Tao
Physics pg 307-8)  
  
Biochemist Alfred Taylor wrote: "A consequence of the
principle that all manifestation, all matter, whether in the
category of minerals or as the tissues and organs of living
creatures, is derived from a common source-substance leads
directly to the notion that the quality of meaning - or
noumenon, consciousness as such - is as the basis of
phenomenon.  Since all forms have their origin in one source,
we are forced to the conclusion that organization is the
determining factor, whether energy appears as hydrogen or
lead, as a daisy or a man.  Something must distinguish one
from the other, and that something is organization, meaning,
consciousness." (Alfred Taylor, "Meaning and Matter,"
Consciousness and Reality,  Edited by Charles Muses and Arthur
M. Young, (New York: Avon Books, 1972), pg. 172.)  
  
QUANTUM DYNAMICS AND INDETERMINACY SUGGEST FREE CHOICE   
  
* There is great uncertainty of how subatomic particles.
suggesting they are consciously choosing how to act. 
Indeterminacy seems to be more like self-determination.      
     -- arbitrary creation and destruction of particles;  
     -- the unpredictable release of quantum packages??, the
     leaps from one quantum state to another;   
     -- photons changing between being particles and waves;  
     -- "virtual transitions", where subatomic waves and
     particles seem to explore a multitude of possibilities,
     sending out "temporary feelers", before choosing any
     particle form or leaping from one quantum state to
     another.  (The "Many Worlds" theory draws on this,
     suggesting there are even a number of worlds where each
     of us could find different versions of our own selves.) 
     
  
*  It has been found that unobserved electrons, which seem to
be both waves and particles at the same time, choose to become
either waves or particles at the moment a scientist observes
them.  Something about the act of observing or measuring the
quantum wave actually makes it "collapse" into one of it's
many possible states and become a single, fixed actuality or
entity.  Some scientists interpret this as mean that reality
does not become real until we look at it.  Others think this
is because electrons, knowing we are expecting to see them in
some specific form, choose to accommodate us by appearing in
that form.  
  
*  In scientific experiments, subatomic particles seem to
choose to change form in response to experimental conditions. 
 
     -- In the "tunnel effect" a particle becomes a wave in
     order to pass through an impenetrable object, then
     becomes a particle again;  
     -- In the "double slit experiment" photons behave
     differently, depending on whether they are passed through
     a plate with one or two slits.  If there is just one
     slit, they act like particles, but if there are two slits
     they act like waves.  
  
* Feynman and electrons moving back and forth in time - is it
between memory and fantasy, creating reality??  This may be
the process of memory and imagination.  
  
1970 - Physicist Evan Harris Walker in article "The Nature of
